## Priority 2: Add AsNoTracking() to All Read-Only Queries

### Problem: Unnecessary Change Tracking Overhead

**Symptoms:**
- High memory usage during queries
- Slow query execution even for simple queries
- Data being used only for reading/display
- No updates/deletes after query

**Solution:**
Add `.AsNoTracking()` immediately after `context.Set<T>()`

### Pattern

**❌ BEFORE:**
```csharp
var risks = await context.Set<Risk>()
    .Include(r => r.Category)
    .Include(r => r.Type)
    .Where(r => r.EntityId == id)
    .ToListAsync();

var stakeholders = await context.Set<Stakeholder>()
    .Include(s => s.User)
    .Where(s => s.EntityId == id)
    .ToListAsync();
```

**✅ AFTER:**
```csharp
var risks = await context.Set<Risk>()
    .AsNoTracking() // ✅ Added - no updates will be made
    .Include(r => r.Category)
    .Include(r => r.Type)
    .Where(r => r.EntityId == id)
    .ToListAsync();

var stakeholders = await context.Set<Stakeholder>()
    .AsNoTracking() // ✅ Added - read-only query
    .Include(s => s.User)
    .Where(s => s.EntityId == id)
    .ToListAsync();
```

### When to Use AsNoTracking()

✅ **USE for:**
- Read-only queries (no updates/deletes after loading)
- Data formatting/transformation operations
- API response generation
- Report generation
- Export operations
- AI/ML data processing

❌ **DON'T USE for:**
- Queries where you'll update entities after loading
- Queries followed by `SaveChanges()`
- Entities that need change tracking for audit

