## Priority 4: Parallel Query Execution with DbContextFactory

### Problem: Sequential Query Execution

**Symptoms:**
- Multiple independent queries execute one after another
- Total execution time = sum of all query times
- CPU and database are underutilized during sequential waits

### Prerequisites

1. **Verify DbContextFactory is registered in Startup.cs:**
```csharp
// In ConfigureDataAccess method
services.AddDbContextFactory<YourDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseNpgsql(connectionString));
```

2. **Verify connection pool settings support concurrency:**
```csharp
var connectionStringBuilder = new NpgsqlConnectionStringBuilder(connectionString)
{
    MinPoolSize = 10,              // Keep connections warm
    MaxPoolSize = 100,             // Allow concurrent connections
    Multiplexing = true,           // Enable multiplexing
    // ... other settings
};
```

### Implementation Pattern

**Step 1: Add DbContextFactory to Manager Constructor**

```csharp
public class YourManager : BaseManager
{
    private readonly IMapper mapper;
    private readonly AppDbContext context;
    private readonly IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext> _dbContextFactory; // ✅ Add this
    
    public YourManager(
        IMapper mapper,
        AppDbContext context,
        IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext> dbContextFactory, // ✅ Add parameter
        IConfiguration configuration,
        IPermissionService permissionService = null,
        IHttpContextAccessor httpContextAccessor = null,
        IServiceProvider serviceProvider = null)
    {
        this.mapper = mapper;
        this.context = context;
        this._dbContextFactory = dbContextFactory; // ✅ Store factory
        // ... other initializations
    }
}
```

**Step 2: Update ManagerWrapper Instantiation**

```csharp
// In ManagerWrapper.cs
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore; // ✅ Add this using

// In constructor:
var dbContextFactory = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService<IDbContextFactory<YourDbContext>>();
yourManager = new YourManager(mapper, context, dbContextFactory, configuration, 
    permissionService, httpContextAccessor, serviceProvider);
```

**Step 3: Implement Parallel Query Execution**

**❌ BEFORE (Sequential Execution):**
```csharp
public async Task<DetailedModel> GetDetailedDataAsync(int id)
{
    // Total time = T1 + T2 + T3 + T4 + T5 (e.g., 50 seconds)
    var collection1 = await context.Set<Collection1>()...ToListAsync(); // 10s
    var collection2 = await context.Set<Collection2>()...ToListAsync(); // 10s
    var collection3 = await context.Set<Collection3>()...ToListAsync(); // 10s
    var collection4 = await context.Set<Collection4>()...ToListAsync(); // 10s
    var collection5 = await context.Set<Collection5>()...ToListAsync(); // 10s
    
    return MapToModel(collection1, collection2, collection3, collection4, collection5);
}
```

**✅ AFTER (Parallel Execution):**
```csharp
public async Task<DetailedModel> GetDetailedDataAsync(int id)
{
    // ==========================================
    // QUERY 1: Main entity (must run first - not parallelized)
    // ==========================================
    var entity = await context.Set<Entity>()
        .AsNoTracking()
        .Include(e => e.SimpleNavigation)
        .FirstOrDefaultAsync(e => e.Id == id);
    
    if (entity == null)
        throw new KeyNotFoundException($"Entity with ID {id} not found");
    
    // ==========================================
    // PARALLEL WAVE 1: Execute independent queries concurrently
    // Each task uses its own DbContext instance for thread safety
    // Total time = MAX(T1, T2, T3, T4, T5) (e.g., 10 seconds instead of 50!)
    // ==========================================
    var task1 = Task.Run(async () => 
    {
        await using var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
        return await ctx.Set<Collection1>()
            .AsNoTracking()
            .Where(c => c.EntityId == id)
            .Include(c => c.Related)
            .ToListAsync();
    });
    
    var task2 = Task.Run(async () => 
    {
        await using var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
        return await ctx.Set<Collection2>()
            .AsNoTracking()
            .Where(c => c.EntityId == id)
            .Include(c => c.Related)
            .ToListAsync();
    });
    
    var task3 = Task.Run(async () => 
    {
        await using var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
        return await ctx.Set<Collection3>()
            .AsNoTracking()
            .Where(c => c.EntityId == id)
            .ToListAsync();
    });
    
    var task4 = Task.Run(async () => 
    {
        await using var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
        return await ctx.Set<Collection4>()
            .AsNoTracking()
            .Where(c => c.EntityId == id)
            .ToListAsync();
    });
    
    var task5 = Task.Run(async () => 
    {
        await using var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
        return await ctx.Set<Collection5>()
            .AsNoTracking()
            .Where(c => c.EntityId == id)
            .ToListAsync();
    });
    
    // Wait for ALL parallel queries to complete
    await Task.WhenAll(task1, task2, task3, task4, task5);
    
    // Get results (tasks already completed)
    var collection1 = await task1;
    var collection2 = await task2;
    var collection3 = await task3;
    var collection4 = await task4;
    var collection5 = await task5;
    
    // ==========================================
    // PARALLEL WAVE 2: Dependent queries (if needed)
    // Execute queries that depend on Wave 1 results
    // ==========================================
    var ids1 = collection1.Select(c => c.Id).ToList();
    var ids2 = collection2.Select(c => c.Id).ToList();
    
    if (ids1.Any() || ids2.Any())
    {
        var dependentTask1 = ids1.Any() ? Task.Run(async () => 
        {
            await using var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
            return await ctx.Set<DependentCollection1>()
                .AsNoTracking()
                .Where(d => ids1.Contains(d.ParentId))
                .ToListAsync();
        }) : Task.FromResult(new List<DependentCollection1>());
        
        var dependentTask2 = ids2.Any() ? Task.Run(async () => 
        {
            await using var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
            return await ctx.Set<DependentCollection2>()
                .AsNoTracking()
                .Where(d => ids2.Contains(d.ParentId))
                .ToListAsync();
        }) : Task.FromResult(new List<DependentCollection2>());
        
        await Task.WhenAll(dependentTask1, dependentTask2);
        
        var dependent1 = await dependentTask1;
        var dependent2 = await dependentTask2;
        
        // Assign dependent collections...
    }
    
    // Assign collections to entity
    entity.Collection1 = collection1;
    entity.Collection2 = collection2;
    entity.Collection3 = collection3;
    entity.Collection4 = collection4;
    entity.Collection5 = collection5;
    
    return MapToModel(entity);
}
```

### Critical Rules for Parallel Execution

1. **✅ DO:**
   - Use `Task.Run()` to ensure tasks execute on thread pool
   - Create separate DbContext for each parallel task using factory
   - Use `await using` for automatic DbContext disposal
   - Always add `.AsNoTracking()` to queries
   - Group independent queries in Wave 1, dependent queries in Wave 2
   - Use `Task.WhenAll()` to wait for all parallel tasks

2. **❌ DON'T:**
   - Share DbContext across parallel tasks (NOT thread-safe!)
   - Parallelize queries that depend on each other
   - Forget to dispose DbContext (memory leak!)
   - Use parallel execution if only 2-3 simple queries
   - Parallelize the main entity query (it must run first)

### When to Use Parallel Execution

**✅ Good Candidates:**
- 5+ independent queries
- Each query takes 1+ seconds
- Queries don't depend on each other's results
- System has available CPU/database capacity

**❌ Poor Candidates:**
- Only 2-3 simple queries
- Queries are very fast (<100ms each)
- Queries have complex dependencies
- Limited database connection pool
- Sequential execution time is already acceptable

