## Common Pitfalls to Avoid

### ❌ Pitfall 1: Forgetting AsNoTracking on New Queries
```csharp
// ❌ WRONG - Added query but forgot AsNoTracking
var newCollection = await context.Set<Entity>()
    .Where(e => e.ParentId == id)
    .ToListAsync();

// ✅ CORRECT - Always add AsNoTracking for read-only
var newCollection = await context.Set<Entity>()
    .AsNoTracking()
    .Where(e => e.ParentId == id)
    .ToListAsync();
```

### ❌ Pitfall 2: Sharing DbContext in Parallel Tasks
```csharp
// ❌ WRONG - Sharing context (NOT thread-safe!)
var task1 = Task.Run(() => context.Set<Entity1>()...ToListAsync());
var task2 = Task.Run(() => context.Set<Entity2>()...ToListAsync());
await Task.WhenAll(task1, task2); // ❌ Can cause corruption!

// ✅ CORRECT - Separate context per task
var task1 = Task.Run(async () => {
    await using var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
    return await ctx.Set<Entity1>()...ToListAsync();
});
var task2 = Task.Run(async () => {
    await using var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
    return await ctx.Set<Entity2>()...ToListAsync();
});
await Task.WhenAll(task1, task2); // ✅ Thread-safe
```

### ❌ Pitfall 3: Not Disposing DbContext in Parallel Tasks
```csharp
// ❌ WRONG - Forgot to dispose (memory leak!)
var task1 = Task.Run(async () => {
    var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
    return await ctx.Set<Entity>()...ToListAsync();
    // ❌ Context never disposed!
});

// ✅ CORRECT - Auto-dispose with await using
var task1 = Task.Run(async () => {
    await using var ctx = await _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
    return await ctx.Set<Entity>()...ToListAsync();
}); // ✅ Context disposed automatically
```

### ❌ Pitfall 4: Parallelizing Dependent Queries
```csharp
// ❌ WRONG - Task2 depends on Task1's result
var task1 = Task.Run(async () => { /* Load parents */ });
var task2 = Task.Run(async () => { /* Load children using parent IDs */ });
await Task.WhenAll(task1, task2); // ❌ Task2 will fail!

// ✅ CORRECT - Execute in waves
// Wave 1: Load parents
var parents = await LoadParentsAsync();
var parentIds = parents.Select(p => p.Id).ToList();

// Wave 2: Load children (depends on Wave 1)
var children = await LoadChildrenAsync(parentIds);
```

